    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notger">Notger</a> (972–1008) turned the see of Liège into a territorial principality of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-fexhe">Peace of Fexhe</a> of 1316 bound the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> to change the law only with the consent of the estates.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> and arms making made it rich; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liege-revolution">Liège Revolution</a> of 1789 and French annexation in 1795 ended it.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Feast of Corpus Christi</a> began in Liège, instituted by its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in 1246 after the visions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juliana-of-cornillon">Juliana of Cornillon</a>, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-iv">Pope Urban IV</a> extended it to the whole church.</p></aside>
    </section>

