    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lambert-of-italy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lambert-of-italy.jpg" alt="Lambert of Italy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lambert-of-italy">Lambert of Italy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Four_kings_of_Italy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Johannes Berardi (chronicon casauriense), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The bishopric that became Liège descended from the late Roman see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongeren">Tongeren</a>, which moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a> and then, after the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lambert-of-italy">Lambert</a> at Liège around 705, to the place of his martyrdom, where his successor Hubert established the see in the early eighth century. Liège lay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> in the old Carolingian heartland, and after Lotharingia joined the German kingdom its bishops became pillars of the Ottonian imperial church, whose emperors relied on loyal prelates rather than hereditary dukes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notger">Notger</a>, a Swabian cleric appointed bishop in 972, turned the diocese into a principality. In 980 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-ii-duke-of-bavaria">Otto II</a> issued a diploma confirming the immunity of the church of Liège, removing its lands from the jurisdiction of royal counts, and in 985 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Otto III</a> granted Notger the county of Huy, the first of many counties acquired by the see. Notger enclosed Liège with walls, rebuilt the cathedral of Saint Lambert, endowed collegiate churches and expanded the cathedral schools, which made Liège one of the leading intellectual centres of the empire. A later verse held that Liège owed Notger to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Christ</a> and everything else to Notger. His successors gathered further counties and rights, and in 1096 Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otbert">Otbert</a> bought the castle and lordship of Bouillon from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a>, who needed money for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/borgloon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/borgloon.jpg" alt="Borgloon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/borgloon">Borgloon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borgloon_Stadhuis_3.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Torsade de Pointes, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The principality straddled a language frontier: most of its people spoke Walloon, a dialect of French, but the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgloon">Loon</a>, annexed in 1366, spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> dialects. The city of Liège grew at the junction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ourthe">Ourthe</a>, and the Meuse valley was a corridor of trade between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, Champagne and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. Mosan craftsmen were renowned for metalwork: the bronze baptismal font cast by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renier-de-huy">Renier de Huy</a> in the early twelfth century, now in the church of Saint Bartholomew, is a masterpiece of Romanesque art. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> was mined around Liège from about 1200, among the earliest coal workings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and fed metalworking, nail-making and later a great arms industry; by the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries Liège and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herstal">Herstal</a> were among Europe’s largest producers of firearms, selling to armies on all sides. Religious life was intense: the beguine movement had early roots in the diocese, and the visions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juliana-of-cornillon">Juliana of Cornillon</a> led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-of-thourotte">Robert of Thourotte</a> to institute the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Feast of Corpus Christi</a> in 1246, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-iv">Pope Urban IV</a>, a former archdeacon of Liège, extended to the whole church in 1264. The springs of Spa, within the principality, made it a fashionable resort for European aristocrats in the eighteenth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-steppes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-steppes.jpg" alt="Battle of Steppes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-steppes">Battle of Steppes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stepsheuvel_16-04-2008_13-34-59.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paul Hermans, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As an ecclesiastical state the principality relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, town militias and hired troops rather than a standing army, and its frontier with Brabant, the most expansionist of its neighbours, saw repeated war. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-steppes">Battle of Steppes</a> in 1213 the militia of Liège and its allies defeated Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-i-duke-of-brabant">Henry I of Brabant</a>, a victory long celebrated in the city. The townsmen were equally ready to fight their own princes. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-othee">Battle of Othée</a> in 1408 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-fearless">John the Fearless</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, and his allies crushed a Liège army that had rebelled against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>-elect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-duke-of-bavaria">John of Bavaria</a>, and thousands of Liègeois were killed. Burgundian pressure grew under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a>, whose forces sacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-working town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinant">Dinant</a> in 1466. Liège rose again, was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-brustem">Battle of Brustem</a> in 1467 and, after a final rising, was taken and sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> in 1468; according to tradition six hundred men of Franchimont made a desperate night attack on the duke’s camp before the city fell. Liège was burned for weeks and its walls torn down. In the early modern period the principality sought safety in neutrality, but the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> and his enemies repeatedly crossed its territory, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough">Marlborough</a> captured the city and its citadel in 1702 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-de-bourbon-bishop-of-liege"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-de-bourbon-bishop-of-liege.jpg" alt="Louis de Bourbon (bishop of Liège)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-de-bourbon-bishop-of-liege">Louis de Bourbon (bishop of Liège)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_de_Bourbon(Couleurs).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SirachMatthews, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> was elected by the cathedral chapter of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lambert-of-italy">Lambert</a>, usually under imperial or papal influence, and ruled both a diocese that stretched far beyond his principality and a temporal state within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, later assigned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-rhenish-westphalian-circle">Lower Rhenish–Westphalian Circle</a>. Power was shared. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-fexhe">Peace of Fexhe</a> of 1316 declared that the law could be changed only with the consent of the “sense of the country”, represented by the three estates: the cathedral chapter, the nobility and the “good towns” led by Liège. In 1373 the Tribunal of the Twenty-Two was created to judge officials of the prince who broke the law, and in the fourteenth century the city’s craft <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> won a dominant voice in municipal government. Bishops and towns struggled over taxation and jurisdiction for centuries, and outside powers intervened: the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> imposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-de-bourbon-bishop-of-liege">Louis de Bourbon</a> as bishop in 1456, and from 1581 to 1688 members of the Bavarian house of Wittelsbach, who were also archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, held the see. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-henry-of-bavaria">Maximilian Henry of Bavaria</a> ended the guilds’ political power with the Regulation of 1684. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erard-de-la-marck">Erard de la Marck</a>, prince-bishop from 1505 to 1538, rebuilt the Palace of the Prince-Bishops and pursued a policy of neutrality between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> that his successors maintained, with interruptions, into the eighteenth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/stavelot-triptych"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stavelot-triptych.jpg" alt="Stavelot Triptych" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/stavelot-triptych">Stavelot Triptych</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stavelot.Triptych.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Original File Uploaded by en:User:Stbalbach, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Liège was one of the main channels of learning and art in the medieval empire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notger">Notger</a>’s schools trained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and scholars for the imperial church, and in the eleventh century Liège was celebrated as a northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. The Mosan school of goldsmiths and metalworkers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> valley produced works such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stavelot-triptych">Stavelot Triptych</a> and influenced Romanesque art across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the brass and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> ware of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinant">Dinant</a> gave its name to <em>dinanderie</em>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Feast of Corpus Christi</a>, first celebrated in Liège, became one of the major festivals of the Catholic church. The composer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-ciconia">Johannes Ciconia</a>, born in Liège in the fourteenth century, and the eighteenth-century opera composer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-gretry">André-Ernest-Modeste Grétry</a> carried Liège music abroad, and in 1756 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-rousseau-journalist">Pierre Rousseau</a> founded the <em>Journal encyclopédique</em> in Liège, an important organ of the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, which later moved to Bouillon. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mines and arms workshops made the Liège region one of the first areas of continental Europe to industrialise, and after the principality’s end it became a core of Belgian heavy industry, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-cockerill">Cockerill</a> works at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seraing">Seraing</a> occupying the former summer palace of the prince-bishops. The tradition of civic liberty embodied in the Peace of Fexhe and the revolution of 1789 remains part of Walloon identity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jemappes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-jemappes.jpg" alt="Battle of Jemappes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jemappes">Battle of Jemappes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_Jemmapes,_6_novembre_1792.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Eighteenth-century Liège was prosperous but politically restless. Enlightened prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-charles-de-velbruck">François-Charles de Velbruck</a> (1772–1784) encouraged education and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/societe-libre-d-emulation">Société d’Émulation</a>, founded in 1779, but his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantin-francois-de-hoensbroeck">Constantin-François de Hoensbroeck</a> faced growing opposition, sharpened by a dispute over the licensing of gaming houses at Spa. On 18 August 1789, weeks after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storming-of-the-bastille">Bastille</a>, citizens of Liège seized the town hall and installed new magistrates; Hoensbroeck soon fled, and the revolutionaries proclaimed a republic. The imperial chamber court ordered his restoration, and after Prussian mediation failed, Austrian troops reoccupied Liège in January 1791. The restored regime lasted less than two years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-occupation-of-the-netherlands">French revolutionary armies</a> entered the city after their victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jemappes">Jemappes</a> in November 1792, and the citizens voted for union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> returned in March 1793. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fleurus-1794">Battle of Fleurus</a> in June 1794 the French came back for good, and the cathedral of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lambert-of-italy">Lambert</a>, symbol of the old order, was demolished from 1794. On 1 October 1795 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> annexed the principality to France, dividing it chiefly between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ourthe">Ourthe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse-inferieure">Meuse-Inférieure</a>. The settlement of 1815 assigned its lands to the United <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands</a>, and after Belgian independence they were divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and the Netherlands in 1839.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Prince-Bishopric of Liège shows how a church principality of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> survived for eight centuries between powerful neighbours, combining an early charter of shared government with its estates, a great centre of medieval learning and Mosan art, and one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and arms industries.</em></p>

